WEBVTT

00:00:01.156 --> 00:00:03.414
Some people wait for the perfect setup.

00:00:03.484 --> 00:00:05.395
Arjun doesn't.

00:00:05.684 --> 00:00:08.759
Introducing Arjun Mishra.

00:00:08.956 --> 00:00:10.850
He wanted to build for iPhone.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.232
He didn't own a Mac.

00:00:12.302 --> 00:00:14.414
So the cloud became his Mac.

00:00:14.484 --> 00:00:19.141
On GitHub's macOS runners, his app is built, tested, signed,

00:00:19.211 --> 00:00:22.850
and shipped to the App Store, all from a Windows PC.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.068
That app is Roadpack: one live trip for every car in the group,

00:00:27.138 --> 00:00:30.632
kept in sync in real time, even when the signal drops.

00:00:30.702 --> 00:00:33.614
And it lives where you look: in Live Activities,

00:00:33.684 --> 00:00:36.741
the Dynamic Island, widgets, and Siri.

00:00:36.811 --> 00:00:38.050
Three releases in,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.559
a paying subscriber within the first six downloads, before any marketing.

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:44.668
Then he co-founded Mune,

00:00:44.738 --> 00:00:48.595
a finance app where every AI answer happens on your iPhone.

00:00:48.665 --> 00:00:53.468
With Apple's MLX, a 4-billion-parameter model runs right on the device.

00:00:53.538 --> 00:00:56.086
And it doesn't just answer. It acts.

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:59.941
Checking what you can afford, building budgets, setting goals,

00:01:00.011 --> 00:01:01.750
all from one conversation.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.241
At UBC, his research asks a bigger question.

00:01:05.311 --> 00:01:08.150
Can AI agents check the web for accessibility?

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.077
His forty agents found 86% of real issues.

00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:14.623
A standard checker? 36%.

00:01:14.693 --> 00:01:18.677
First author. Submitted to CHI 2027.

00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:23.105
At IFS Copperleaf, his internship ended with an offer:

00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:26.114
keep building, part-time, through his final year.

00:01:26.184 --> 00:01:31.495
Data pipelines, and a return-on-investment portal for senior leadership.

00:01:31.565 --> 00:01:33.550
And away from the keyboard?

00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:38.732
He flies over Fuji and Vancouver's fireworks.

00:01:38.802 --> 00:01:40.786
He hikes. Builds LEGO Technic.

00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:43.914
And has two hackathon prizes.

00:01:45.253 --> 00:01:46.887
Put it all together.

00:01:46.957 --> 00:01:51.036
A computer science student at UBC, with a minor in commerce,

00:01:51.106 --> 00:01:54.554
two apps on the App Store, shipped from a Windows PC,

00:01:54.624 --> 00:01:57.238
research that makes AI show its work,

00:01:57.308 --> 00:01:58.992
and two papers submitted to CHI.

00:01:59.342 --> 00:02:01.403
More than 250 students taught,

00:02:01.473 --> 00:02:03.809
and a company that asked him to stay.

00:02:03.879 --> 00:02:05.778
He takes an idea all the way,

00:02:05.848 --> 00:02:09.131
from the design, to the code, to the people who use it.

00:02:09.201 --> 00:02:10.587
Give him a hard problem.

00:02:10.657 --> 00:02:12.051
He'll find a way to ship it.

00:02:12.121 --> 00:02:14.583
No perfect setup required.

00:02:14.653 --> 00:02:16.158
Arjun Mishra.

00:02:16.228 --> 00:02:17.835
Available May 2027,

00:02:18.859 --> 00:02:24.254
for software, forward-deployed, and AI engineering roles.
